  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b5283855",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Realized Volatility Bayesian Prediction - clean notebook\n",
    "\n",
    "This notebook studies whether realized volatility can be predicted using HAR-type regression models and Bayesian extensions. Volatility is notoriously difficult to forecast, especially during sudden market stress, so simple point predictions are expected to be limited. We first show how standard linear HAR models behave, then move to log-volatility, jump-regime features, and Bayesian models.\n",
    "\n",
    "The main idea is not only to predict the exact future realized volatility, but also to build a warning framework. Instead of asking whether the model can perfectly forecast every volatility spike, we ask whether the Bayesian posterior predictive distribution can identify periods where high volatility becomes plausible.\n",
    "\n",
    "The flow is intentionally notebook-like. We want to take the reader through the same thinking process as the analysis developed:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. download market prices,\n",
    "2. construct realized variance,\n",
    "3. fit a basic HAR model,\n",
    "4. try a jump-regime HAR extension,\n",
    "5. switch to a log-HAR specification,\n",
    "6. compare naive benchmarks,\n",
    "7. fit Bayesian HAR models with Normal and Student-t likelihoods,\n",
    "8. use posterior predictive intervals and probabilities for high-volatility warning signals.\n"
   ]
  },
